<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Taghlib</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 979</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Taghlib was the Hamdanid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> who deposed his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a> in 967. He became entangled in the struggles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>, backing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. In 978 Adud al-Dawla expelled him from Mosul, and he was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in 979 while fighting forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born in 940, Abu Taghlib was the eldest son of the Hamdanid al-Hasan, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a>, and of a Kurdish woman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> bint Ahmad. His father ruled a practically independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> over the Jazira from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> but had been forced to accept Buyid overlordship and pay tribute. Abu Taghlib first appears in 964, when he led resistance to the Buyid Mu'izz al-Dawla after Mosul was occupied. With the tacit consent of most of his brothers he deposed his father in May 967 and held him in the fortress of Ardamusht until his death in February 969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His half-brother Abu'l-Muzzafar Hamdan contested his rule at once, and Abu Taghlib turned to the Buyid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar</a>, for help. He prevailed, and by 971 held all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-bakr">Diyar Bakr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar">Diyar Mudar</a>, uniting the Jazira; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muti">al-Muti</a> even granted him suzerainty over the Hamdanid emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Relations with Bakhtiyar swung between war and alliance: after a campaign in 973 a settlement of 974 gave Abu Taghlib the title Uddat al-Dawla in return for tribute, and his help to Bakhtiyar during the revolt of Sabuktakin freed him from that tribute in 975.</p><p>He also faced Byzantine raids under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> in 972 and 974, paying tribute to the emperor for a time, and in 976 he supplied light cavalry to the rebel general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardas-skleros">Bardas Skleros</a>. In November 977 Bakhtiyar was driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> by his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. Abu Taghlib allied with him in exchange for the surrender of Hamdan, who was executed, but the two were defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in May 978 and Adud al-Dawla took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abu Taghlib fled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>, sought help from Skleros and then from his sister Jamila at Rahba, while two of his brothers took service with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>. In southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he tried in vain to win the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, held by the rebel al-Qassam. The Fatimid general Fadl promised him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>, but when Abu Taghlib attacked it in August 979, Fadl's troops aided its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mufarrij-ibn-daghfal">Mufarrij ibn Daghfal ibn al-Jarrah</a>; Abu Taghlib was captured and executed on 29 August, likely at the wish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. The Jazira later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Taghlib” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nasir al-Dawla was deposed by his son <b>Abu Taghlib</b> in 967.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul">Hamdanid emirate of Mosul</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.93,34.86&amp;year=969" aria-label="Ramla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Abu Taghlib"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5352 4828h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5352 4828h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.93,34.86&amp;year=969">What was here in 969 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
